WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.620
The United States spends about $50 billion
a year on foreign aid.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.260
That sounds like a lot, doesn’t it?

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:10.820
So it’s not surprising that Americans ask
why their government sends so much money abroad

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.100
when there are pressing needs at home.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.060
But is aid simply charity?

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.240
Or does it serve U.S. interests?

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.460
Foreign aid has long been a vital part of
U.S. foreign policy.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:27.019
It takes on hunger and it improves health.

00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:31.510
It builds schools, creates jobs, it trains
law enforcement.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.560
It encourages other countries to support our
goals.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.420
And it has traditionally enjoyed bipartisan
support in Washington.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.340
That is until recently.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.380
Many now question its purpose and its expense
for Americans.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.289
Let’s go back to those numbers.

00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:49.580
$50 billion dollars is a lot of money.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:53.540
But it’s actually just 1 percent of the
total federal budget.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.680
By comparison, defense spending is over $700
billion dollars.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.920
That’s 17% of the federal budget.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.980
In sheer dollar terms, the U.S. is the world’s
largest aid donor.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.380
But the U.S. is also the world's largest
economy.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:11.200
Many countries give a lot more than the U.S.
as a percentage of their economies.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.240
Now let’s see where that money goes.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.860
Some of the biggest outlays go to fighting
disease.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.520
U.S. aid has been a gamechanger in the fight
against HIV and AIDS around the world.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.280
Its investment of over $90 billion dollars
is credited with saving millions of lives

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.580
and preventing the disease from spreading
further.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:34.120
The United States also spends a lot to help when natural disasters strike or when wars force people to flee their homes.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:39.760
U.S. foreign aid supports international organizations
like the UN Development Program or World Bank.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.320
They work on everything from reducing infant
deaths to building highways and ports in poorer countries.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.740
And the United States funds programs to promote
free-markets and democracy abroad.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.120
But here’s something you might not realize.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:56.020
Military and security aid is also part of
that $50 billion dollars.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.860
Much of this money goes to help allies with
equipment and training.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:04.860
Iraq, Afghanistan, Israel, Egypt, and Jordan
receive the most support.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.160
So, is foreign aid expensive?

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.340
Sure.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.980
But it’s also an investment.

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:13.020
That $50 billion dollars nurtures alliances.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.820
It promotes democracy, trade, and economic growth.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:20.440
It helps prevent conflicts that might lead
to military intervention.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.480
It can even fight terrorism.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:29.420
As the Joint Chiefs of the U.S. Military have argued, “diplomacy and development are a hell of a lot cheaper than defense.”

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.440
As an investment, foreign aid is quite cost-effective.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.900
But providing international aid isn’t just
about the costs.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.040
It's vital for a global leader.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.460
If that still is a role the United States
wants to play.

